        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best charging station for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a nightstand in an Apple household, the Anker 3-in-1 Cube with MagSafe is the most popular pick because it delivers full MagSafe speed from a small folding body. For a desk with a laptop, the UGREEN Nexode 100W desktop charger or the Anker 727 makes more sense. The right answer depends on whether you need wireless pads, AC outlets or laptop wattage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a charging station better than separate chargers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a household with several devices, usually yes. One station replaces three or four bricks, frees up outlets, and keeps cables in one place. The tradeoff is shared wattage: each device may charge a little slower when everything is plugged in. For overnight charging that rarely matters. For a laptop in daytime use, size the station so its port still gets full power.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a charging station charge a laptop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wired USB-C stations can, as long as one port delivers the wattage your laptop's own charger is rated for, typically 30 to 100 watts and up to 140 watts for a 16-inch MacBook Pro. Wireless stations cannot charge laptops at all. Check the station's per-port maximum with other devices connected, and use a cable rated for the wattage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between Qi2 and MagSafe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                MagSafe is Apple's magnetic wireless charging system for iPhones. Qi2 is the open industry standard built on that magnetic design, so Qi2 chargers snap onto iPhones and onto Android phones that support Qi2. Both charge at up to 15 watts in their common versions. Generic magnetic chargers without either certification usually charge more slowly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to leave phones on a charging station overnight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with a certified station from a reputable brand. Modern phones manage their own charging and slow down near full, and many offer optimized charging that holds at 80 percent until morning. Keep the station on a hard surface with airflow, use undamaged cables, and replace anything that gets very hot, smells or shows a discolored plug.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a charging station from a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Plug the station's AC plug into the power station's outlet, or plug devices directly into the power station's USB-C ports, which is more efficient. A charging station draws little power, so a mid-size unit can keep phones charged for days. Never connect a power station or generator to a wall outlet to power the house; that is backfeeding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a portable charging station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The phrase is used two ways. Sometimes it means a folding charger like the Anker 3-in-1 Cube that travels well but still plugs into a wall. More often it means a battery you carry, from a power bank to a portable power station, that charges devices away from an outlet. For outages and camping, the battery type is what you want.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
